00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:43.233
But then also you can do whatever you want,

265
00:10:43.233 --> 00:10:44.670
do what you want.

266
00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:46.876
Please, you can do whatever you want

267
00:10:46.876 --> 00:10:47.709
and be whoever you want.

268
00:10:47.709 --> 00:10:48.542
And--

269
00:10:48.542 --> 00:10:50.323
But a little bit of each of those is probably good.

270
00:10:50.323 --> 00:10:51.640
Absolutely.

271
00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:54.930
And they also, grew up in really

272
00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:56.490
tough environments themselves.

273
00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:58.100
So like, knowing where they came from,

274
00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:59.530
and what their background is,

275
00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:00.880
makes a whole lot of sense.

276
00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.500
So that's sort of where all this originated.

277
00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.282
Is in this space of as a child,

278
00:11:07.282 --> 00:11:12.282
how can how could I have had more than one feeling?

279
00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:16.100
And not only plea I had to please.

280
00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.340
But that came from everywhere.

281
00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:19.930
Well, the people pleasing comes

282
00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:22.360
into play in a big way later down the line,

283
00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.780
we're gonna work our way up to that.

284
00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.373
But at this early age,

285
00:11:28.471 --> 00:11:29.304
was there an expectation,

286
00:11:29.304 --> 00:11:30.860
like, you're gonna be this athlete,

287
00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:32.820
or it was just us kind of the atmosphere

288
00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:33.760
that you were in, right?

289
00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.250
It was just an active environment.

290
00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:39.620
And you were just by dint of being your parents kid,

291
00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.610
like around a swimming pool all the time.

292
00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.850
Yeah, so they dropped us off at 08:00 a.m

293
00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:46.000
pick us up at 09:00 p.m when it closed.

294
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.350
We were at the pool all day long.

295
00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.640
I mean, not totally unsupervised

296
00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:53.360
just running around like pool rats,

297
00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.130
Clark was competing with every 18 year old

298
00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:57.740
as a nine year old, he's like, let's compete,

299
00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:58.573
let's do this.

300
00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:04.420
I was not that way, really, I had a very

301
00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:09.645
more of a calm demeanor with competition.

302
00:12:09.645 --> 00:12:12.560
I wasn't really like a savage competitor,

303
00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:13.970
didn't really have this, like,

304
00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:15.180
I'm gonna do this and win.

305
00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.410
And like, I need to train, 20 hours a day.

306
00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:21.270
And but I had this, I wanna be around my friends.

307
00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:22.527
I enjoy the social aspect of it

308
00:12:22.527 --> 00:12:24.050
and I really liked this feeling

309
00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:26.100
of being a part of a team and a community

310
00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:28.053
and my suits cute,

311
00:12:28.053 --> 00:12:31.440
and all of these different things that I could experience

312
00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.630
as a young girl that felt like

313
00:12:35.097 --> 00:12:37.860
I was just free to be me in that space.

314
00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.580
I could just be me.

315
00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:40.760
That's so interesting,

316
00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:43.360
because that's a typical,

317
00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:46.990
somebody who excels at the highest level, right?

318
00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:49.990
Like you're have this kind of freewheeling approach

319
00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:50.870
to the whole thing.

320
00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:54.960
Was the talent immediately evident from the beginning?

321
00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.620
Or did that how did you grow into that aspect

322
00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.123
of being a true competitor?

323
00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:04.920
I had a lot of talent, as a young girl,

324
00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:09.640
I had a lot of natural, just fluid talent.

325
00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:12.740
I was not a workhorse, I was a racehorse

326
00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:16.370
so I didn't perform in practice.

327
00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:18.580
So when people would see me as a little girl,

328
00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:20.660
they would have never guessed that I was really good,

329
00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.343
'cause I was dog last in practice all the time.

330
00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:25.900
But then you get up on the blocks

331
00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:26.930
on the race and win.

332
00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:29.260
Yeah, I had something innate within me

333
00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:32.010
that I could click on and off very well.

334
00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.740
I didn't have to show that approve that to people,

335
00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.660
I didn't feel the need to do that.

336
00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.400
Swimming felt like this artistic expression

337
00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.420
or something internal that I could do

338
00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:47.080
that felt for one time, in my day,

339
00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:49.860
or in my life, or in my week, I could feel free

340
00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.960
and that I didn't have to do it for anybody else.

341
00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:57.080
That's a very 2020 relationship to sport,

342
00:13:57.080 --> 00:14:00.911
but not a very 1995 relationship to sport.

343
00:14:00.911 --> 00:14:03.960
But that's why I felt so outcast

344
00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.660
with, nobody understood me.

345
00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:08.660
There wasn't a Meet that went by

346
00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:12.750
as a child, in high school and college,

347
00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:14.750
to where I really felt seen

348
00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:17.240
for how I performed and the way that I trained

349
00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:20.330
and the way that I saw my relationship with the sport.

350
00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:22.370
I wasn't obsessed with times,

351
00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.140
I wasn't obsessed with stats,

352
00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:25.940
I didn't know any of that.

353
00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:27.160
My brother was that.

354
00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:29.280
My brother was like, boom, boom, boom, time.

355
00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:30.113
Here's your split,

356
00:14:30.113 --> 00:14:31.770
but I'm like, I can't.

357
00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:33.940
I'm trying to visualize what the communication

358
00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:36.027
would have been like with your coaches at the time then

359
00:14:36.027 --> 00:14:39.180
'cause that must have been frustrating.

360
00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:42.220
Very.
Or was it a situation

361
00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.820
in which they're they're trying to jam

362
00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:48.060
this square peg into a round hole

363
00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:49.750
and you're just doing your own thing?

364
00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:51.520
Yeah, Mike De Boer.

365
00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:52.370
Do you know Mike?

366
00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:55.637
Legend--
Likes

367
00:14:55.637 --> 00:14:56.470
(indistinct)

368
00:14:56.470 --> 00:14:57.803
Legend of a coach.

369
00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:00.360
He got me.

370
00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:02.360
It took him a little bit, but he got me.

371
00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:04.510
And so once I got into his group,

372
00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:07.430
so it's like, bronze, silver gold,

373
00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:08.700
pretty senior senior.

374
00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:11.140
Once I got into his senior group,

375
00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.970
it was like, boom,

376
00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:15.160
I could feel seen I was,

377
00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.020
which I know, it's a very cliche term,

378
00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.450
but now looking back,

379
00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.840
I felt so comfortable with him

380
00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:25.110
because he understood the way that I performed

381
00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.610
was in my body, it was a somatic experience of this feeling.

382
00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:32.170
I was a feeling athlete, and I wasn't a thinking athlete

383
00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:35.800
and have the stats and the splits and all that down.

384
00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.320
I had no idea.

385
00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.640
No idea what I was going into at a Meet,

386
00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:41.220
I had no idea what ranking I was,

387
00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:44.386
I had no idea, I'd miss my races all the time.

388
00:15:44.386 --> 00:15:45.219
'Cause I didn't pay attention.

389
00:15:45.219 --> 00:15:47.530
Kind of hilarious and endearing, though,

390
00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:49.880
because swimming is so stats driven,

391
00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:52.370
it's all about, what the pace clock is telling you

392
00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:53.919
and your heart rate

393
00:15:53.919 --> 00:15:57.130
even though kind of the splits that you need to hit

394
00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.940
to get your goal time.

395
00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.960
And what the Olympic Trials qualifying time is,

396
00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:03.940
and all of that stuff is like,

397
00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:05.513
imprinted onto the brain.

398
00:16:06.559 --> 00:16:07.833
But not for you.

399
00:16:08.845 --> 00:16:11.530
And I tried, I tried really hard.

400
00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:14.180
I remember sitting down and writing my goals and trying

401
00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:16.047
to figure out my splits.

402
00:16:16.047 --> 00:16:17.000
And I remember these stories

403
00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:18.970
where I would sit at the kitchen table

404
00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:22.250
at our very first house, Barbary Lane.

405
00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.890
And my parents would sit there and help me,

406
00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:27.090
my mom, of course, had no she was not into that either

407
00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:29.710
but my dad would sit and help me with splits.

408
00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:32.410
And I just did not understand it.

409
00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:34.380
There was no comprehension at all,

410
00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:37.390
as a child like I, it was right over my head.

411
00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:40.650
And I tried, I tried really hard.

412
00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:42.030
And I'm glad that I did

413
00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:44.096
'cause I could see that side of it

414
00:16:44.096 --> 00:16:45.090
that's really important to develop and understand.

415
00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:48.570
But I personally just, it didn't resonate with me.

416
00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:51.400
So I suspect not so good in math class.

417
00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:52.680
Horrible.

418
00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.990
Failed all the way through.

419
00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:56.874
But like, oddly enough, like,

420
00:16:56.874 --> 00:17:00.800
all A's and language arts and art and spelling

421
00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:05.730
and science and social studies and all these other classes,

422
00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.030
but math straight fail.

423
00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:13.030
And I would go to tutors and I, I come home crying so much,

424
00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:15.860
just 'cause I felt like I was a failure.

425
00:17:15.860 --> 00:17:17.800
'Cause I wasn't the same as everybody else.

426
00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.240
My school was a math and science school.

427
00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:23.440
It was heavy in that and I was this artist,

428
00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:24.970
this mind that was free thinking

429
00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:26.270
and saw things abstractly,

430
00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:29.010
and, shapes and all and all these things.

431
00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:32.160
I count things by, like this curtain right here,

432
00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:36.020
I'll count by the number of creases in it, like, I'm weird,

433
00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:38.590
I think of things in a very odd and abstract way

434
00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:40.093
and that was not okay.

435
00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:42.330
When I, that was not okay,

436
00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.310
that was very confusing to people.

437
00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:47.710
And so once I started to realize that,

438
00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:50.250
I started to gear myself more towards things like that

439
00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:51.100
in high school and stuff,

440
00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:54.880
I took art and AP art and stayed in that lane.

441
00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.400
But when it came to swimming,

442
00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.040
I really had to work with Mike

443
00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:59.970
on what that looked like for me,

444
00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.380
because I wasn't the same as everybody else in the pool.

445
00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:03.500
Interesting.

446
00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:05.040
Do you think that your swimming career

447
00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:06.170
would have looked different

448
00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:11.170
if you just stayed under his tutelage the whole time?

449
00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:13.180
Oh, yeah.

450
00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:14.530
'Cause he understood you.

451
00:18:16.015 --> 00:18:17.850
I think there would have been a learning curve

452
00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:20.060
again, and again, and again,

453
00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:21.610
as I went on through the ranks of it.

454
00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:24.530
I would have changed and I would have grown

455
00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:25.880
and I would have gone through XYZ,

456
00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:27.580
and it would have gone over again.

457
00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:30.860
But I think about that all the time,

458
00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:33.670
just like what I could have achieved,

459
00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:35.640
or done differently.

460
00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:38.787
And I suppose that's normal for people to think about.

461
00:18:38.787 --> 00:18:39.620
Sure.

462
00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:43.300
But yeah, having to go to college then

463
00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:47.969
and sort of relearn, or have a coach relearn me

464
00:18:47.969 --> 00:18:51.710
was the whole exhaustion, exhausting process.

465
00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:52.920
We're getting up to that.

466
00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.300
But first was there,

467
00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.840
I'm wondering whether there was like an inflection point

468
00:18:56.840 --> 00:18:58.960
early in your career when you were still in high school

469
00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:02.450
where you were like, oh, man, I'm really good at this.

470
00:19:02.450 --> 00:19:04.040
Was there a race or an event

471
00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:09.040
or something that occurred that dawned upon you

472
00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:13.110
like, I've got a real bright future?

473
00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:17.253
Yes, so I was a breast stroker first.

474
00:19:18.188 --> 00:19:20.181
That's weird, I wouldn't have thought that.

475
00:19:20.181 --> 00:19:22.270
I mean, it was my first Junior National Cut

476
00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:24.150
and my first Senior National Cut.

477
00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:26.620
So how old were you when you first made Junior Nationals?

478
00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:28.610
13.
Yeah, okay.

479
00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:29.900
I didn't go.

480
00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:31.110
Why not?

481
00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:32.793
'Cause I wasn't ready.

482
00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:35.530
According to who.

483
00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:37.990
Me, so I had this--

484
00:19:37.990 --> 00:19:39.110
Who doesn't go?

485
00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:40.347
And that's a big deal
I know.

486
00:19:40.347 --> 00:19:43.253
You make that that qualifying time.

487
00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:49.010
I remember so it was at the University of Kentucky

488
00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:50.670
I made it in 100 breaststroke,

489
00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:52.760
and I got out and I remember my brother was like,

490
00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:55.290
pumping his chest just like happier than I was.

491
00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:56.609
I was like, sick, I made this cut.

492
00:19:56.609 --> 00:19:58.250
Like, I don't even care.

493
00:19:58.250 --> 00:19:59.170
it was this weird thing.

494
00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:00.697
He's like, "Dude, do you know what that means?"

495
00:20:00.697 --> 00:20:01.787
"Do you know what that means,

496
00:20:01.787 --> 00:20:03.827
"you get to go to this Meets in Orlando."

497
00:20:03.827 --> 00:20:04.660
And all this stuff.

498
00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:08.200
And I'm like, no, I'm not going, I start crying on deck.

499
00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.490
And Mike looked at me and he is like,

500
00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:11.670
I could cry right now

501
00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:15.160
and, me, I'm very vulnerable and emotional.

502
00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.090
So thanks for bearing with me.

503
00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:20.957
But he, he looked at me and he goes,

504
00:20:20.957 --> 00:20:22.570
"You don't you don't wanna go?"

505
00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:25.797
And I said, "No, I'm not ready for that yet.

506
00:20:25.797 --> 00:20:27.330
"I'm not there yet."

507
00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:28.797
And he was like, "Okay."

508
00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.830
That's a impressive level of self awareness.

509
00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:34.470
For a young person, though.

510
00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:35.900
I was extremely self aware.

511
00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:36.940
I don't.

512
00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:40.100
But how much of that was just fear of the unknown

513
00:20:40.100 --> 00:20:41.780
verses self understanding.

514
00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:45.370
Certainly fear 100% fear.

515
00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:48.670
But my fear was stemming from going there

516
00:20:48.670 --> 00:20:52.520
and not making people happy if I performed poorly.

517
00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:55.800
So my fear was never that I wouldn't do well,

518
00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:57.123
'cause my fear didn't,

519
00:20:58.700 --> 00:20:59.823
how do I say this?

520
00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:02.872
Like, without sounding weird.

521
00:21:02.872 --> 00:21:06.380
I had a really strong trust in my ability.

522
00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:10.170
I knew I wasn't gonna be a bad swimmer.

523
00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:12.503
My fear was that I wouldn't please people.

524
00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.280
My fear was that I go, and I'd fail,

525
00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:18.240
and people would be upset with me.

526
00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:19.180
And if they're upset with me,

527
00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:20.390
that means I'm a bad person,

528
00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:23.214
and then if I'm a bad person, that means,

529
00:21:23.214 --> 00:21:25.151
whatever else it means.

530
00:21:25.151 --> 00:21:27.670
But it doesn't sound like your parents cared that much,

531
00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:29.580
so it wasn't coming from them was it?

532
00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:31.062
That's the crazy thing,

533
00:21:31.062 --> 00:21:32.890
'cause they were extremely hands off with my swimming.

534
00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:34.410
My mom didn't even know what

535
00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:36.743
like, I'd swim and she would say,

536
00:21:37.717 --> 00:21:40.260
"Oh, my God, that was, it was a great swim."

537
00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:41.933
Mom, I just added 10 seconds,

538
00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:44.160
she had no, she was just you,

539
00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:45.610
but you were the cutest one out there.

540
00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:47.340
I'm like, "Thanks, Mom."

541
00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:48.843
My dad was more in tuned.

542
00:21:50.561 --> 00:21:52.340
But they never made me feel badly

543
00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:54.370
for any of my performances.

544
00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:58.330
So I've started to realize that I was running

545
00:21:58.330 --> 00:22:00.980
from something throughout this time,

546
00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:02.810
and then I kept running, which we'll get to,

547
00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:05.410
but I was running from something

548
00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:06.810
and I think I just have this

549
00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:11.930
and I had this very strong fear of upsetting people

550
00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:15.920
and I still think, I think if you ask anybody that,

551
00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:17.180
where does that come from?

552
00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:19.170
Sure, you can pinpoint a person

553
00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:21.360
or a place or a thing or whatever,

554
00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:23.410
but truthfully, you create a story

555
00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:25.410
about where that comes from.

556
00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.050
You create what that looks like

557
00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:28.610
and then you've had this whole scenario

558
00:22:28.610 --> 00:22:31.330
in your head about why you're not pleasing people.

559
00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:33.613
And I was so imaginative that my stories

560
00:22:33.613 --> 00:22:35.483
were seven ceilings high.

561
00:22:37.515 --> 00:22:39.360
To me, it almost sounds like,

562
00:22:39.360 --> 00:22:43.370
fear of your own strength and power.

563
00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:45.650
That if you were to go to a Meet like that,

564
00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:47.610
and actually excel at the level

565
00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:50.400
that innately you knew you were capable of,

566
00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:52.070
that that would actually be the thing

567
00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:54.100
that would upset people.

568
00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:58.660
Not failing, but actually being as awesome as you knew

569
00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:01.430
that you could be, that might ruffle feathers,

570
00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:02.760
does that fit?

571
00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:06.590
Yeah, you struck a chord just there, for sure.

572
00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:11.590
because upsetting people to me was not being liked, right?

573
00:23:12.730 --> 00:23:14.510
So if I'm not liked,

574
00:23:14.510 --> 00:23:16.560
which is the same thing as what you're saying,

575
00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:19.850
if I beat other girls, if I do great.

576
00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:21.450
What does that mean?

577
00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.040
They don't like me.

578
00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:26.040
And if I'm not liked by these girls,

579
00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:30.350
or by these people, then I'm a bad person.

580
00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:34.270
It all stems back to this, like innate worthiness

581
00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:37.650
within myself that I'm just not a good person

582
00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:38.550
if that happens.

583
00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:40.260
Which is so interesting,

584
00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:42.260
how that manifested at such a young age.

585
00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:44.070
But I, this happened all the time,

586
00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:45.740
I would let people beat me.

587
00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.860
I would literally let people beat me in Meet sometimes

588
00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:49.610
because I was afraid to win.

589
00:23:49.610 --> 00:23:51.920
I'd be looking around and they would be passing me

590
00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:54.170
and I would be like, relieved.

591
00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:55.093
It was crazy.

592
00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:59.120
And really, and then I get to the wall and just be so upset,

593
00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:00.500
'cause I knew that I could have won

594
00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:02.500
and I still have my best races in my life

595
00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.860
I knew I could have gone seven seconds fast on that.

596
00:24:05.860 --> 00:24:08.800
It was more important to you to fit in

597
00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:12.960
than it was to excel at the level you were capable of.

598
00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:13.800
Yeah.

599
00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:15.320
Yeah.

600
00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:17.103
And it's, it's a very,

601
00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.500
it's an intense emotion to revisit.

602
00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:24.780
'Cause, then you think well, what could I have done?

603
00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:26.590
How do you train somebody to not give a shit

604
00:24:26.590 --> 00:24:27.790
about what people think?

605
00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:29.737
How do you catch this?

606
00:24:29.737 --> 00:24:31.250
and you could go down the rabbit hole

607
00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:33.690
of all the ways I could have done it differently.

608
00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:36.850
But the truth of the matter is, as a young woman,

609
00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:40.190
I didn't feel worthy enough.

610
00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:43.650
And bottom line, the issue was I didn't feel worthy enough.

611
00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:46.580
And so that, as a woman, and as a young girl,

612
00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:50.250
I would imagine so many people feel the same way

613
00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:52.250
and I know for a fact.

614
00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:53.983
Everyone is human.
That's the thing.

615
00:24:53.983 --> 00:24:56.840
I mean, some people, maybe the majority

616
00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:59.610
of people listening to this might struggle

617
00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:02.060
to connect with or relate to,

618
00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:03.540
the high highs of your career

619
00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:05.170
and going to the Olympics and all of that,

620
00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:07.890
but that experience that you just related,

621
00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:10.310
I think is highly relatable,

622
00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:14.280
especially particularly to young female athletes.

623
00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.060
Or young females in general,

624
00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:17.540
'cause I don't think it's a thing

625
00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:21.240
that many guys go through.

626
00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:25.150
But you can see it with young girls all over the place.

627
00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:29.676
All over the place, and there's nothing wrong with it,

628
00:25:29.676 --> 00:25:32.590
I think it's just we're catching it faster.

629
00:25:32.590 --> 00:25:34.430
like, we're able to catch it faster,

630
00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:35.740
people are becoming more aware,

631
00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:39.767
coaches can become more aware, parents, friends, peers

632
00:25:39.767 --> 00:25:40.810
can become more aware when someone's like,

633
00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:42.320
I'm not good enough.

634
00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:44.980
I don't want this person to be mad at me.

635
00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:47.180
That's being caught faster, I think

636
00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:48.370
so that these young women

637
00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:51.470
can actually move up a little a little better

638
00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:54.612
and a little stronger and a little more empowered.

639
00:25:54.612 --> 00:25:55.860
And, I say all this and it's,

640
00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:57.360
I'm so human, like,

641
00:25:57.360 --> 00:25:59.878
I don't have a problem with saying,

642
00:25:59.878 --> 00:26:03.370
I knew that I was amazing and also freaked out

643
00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:05.610
that people wouldn't like me.

644
00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:07.110
But yeah, that's so real.

645
00:26:07.110 --> 00:26:09.596
That idea of I'm not good enough,

646
00:26:09.596 --> 00:26:12.593
but also the idea of it's not okay to be too good.

647
00:26:14.703 --> 00:26:15.890
Yeah, too much.

648
00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:18.460
And you still stumble forward,

649
00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:22.003
and continue to excel as you mature as an athlete.

650
00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:24.140
Yeah, it's--
So, that must have been

651
00:26:24.140 --> 00:26:25.973
a little war that you're waging

652
00:26:25.973 --> 00:26:28.460
in your consciousness?

653
00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:32.650
Yeah, it's a trip to think back to when you're young

654
00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:34.460
and to start unpacking.

655
00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:35.990
I don't know, if you've ever done stuff like this,

656
00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:38.890
where you just go back and it's not just the past 10 years,

657
00:26:38.890 --> 00:26:40.960
but it's the 10 before that, and then you start

658
00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:42.133
to realize--
Trust me.

659
00:26:42.133 --> 00:26:44.530
Yeah, I'm pretty sure you have,

660
00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:46.180
just gonna throw out there.

661
00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:51.180
But, it's so fascinating to see the patterns,

662
00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:55.040
and the different things

663
00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:59.800
that you thought were just this problem,

664
00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:01.090
and you need to push down

665
00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:04.370
and when you can finally just say it, and be like,

666
00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:05.890
I was afraid of being too much.

667
00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:07.997
And I was not, I didn't think I was enough.

668
00:27:07.997 --> 00:27:09.420
And just say it right, and like,

669
00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:11.800
get it off your body and let it out of your system.

670
00:27:11.800 --> 00:27:14.550
Its like, whoa, okay I feel better now,

671
00:27:14.550 --> 00:27:15.820
now, where do I start?

672
00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:17.047
Right, and it's confusing--
One year.

673
00:27:17.047 --> 00:27:20.950
'cause those two ideas seem at odds with each other.

674
00:27:20.950 --> 00:27:22.340
How can you possibly harbor

675
00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:24.610
both of those thoughts at the same time?

676
00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:28.010
It goes back to the two thoughts part, right?

677
00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:29.360
I can't have both of those.

678
00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:31.240
So it just must be one.

679
00:27:31.240 --> 00:27:33.360
And I just need to stick with it and deal with it

680
00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:34.800
and shove it down and move on

681
00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:36.600
and keep competing and make everybody happy

682
00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:38.828
and then it's like, whatever.

683
00:27:38.828 --> 00:27:39.917
And that was my mindset, is just like,

684
00:27:39.917 --> 00:27:41.564
well just keep going.

685
00:27:41.564 --> 00:27:45.620
Yeah the alcoholic disposition is,

686
00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:49.180
you feel like, you're superior to everybody

687
00:27:49.180 --> 00:27:51.677
and more than anybody else,

688
00:27:51.677 --> 00:27:54.150
and you're more capable than anyone else.

689
00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:56.840
And at the same time, you're a complete piece of shit,

690
00:27:56.840 --> 00:27:59.600
and you don't deserve anything.

691
00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:04.063
And those two identities coexisting at the same time.

692
00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:07.010
Neither of which are true.

693
00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:09.300
Right, and we create these stories

694
00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:12.180
that becomes such a reality.

695
00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:14.440
And especially if you're an imaginative soul,

696
00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:19.440
and you have all these, little fairy thing in mind.

697
00:28:19.501 --> 00:28:21.670
(indistinct) Autistic sensibility Caroline.

698
00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:24.510
Nothing is in like a linear sequence in your brain.

699
00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:27.800
You've got like, 12 different Alison Wonderlands going on,

700
00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:29.400
So it's like, Oh, that must be true.

701
00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:30.490
Oh, that must be true.

702
00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:31.580
Well, that's true.

703
00:28:31.580 --> 00:28:34.020
And you're picking from all these parts of yourself.

704
00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:37.270
And I think that's where I could, that was a blessing,

705
00:28:37.270 --> 00:28:38.900
but it could also be a curse,

706
00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:42.113
for me as a swimmer and as a human in general.

707
00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:48.230
But it was it, it was a lot to unpack when I finally did,

708
00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:49.800
there's a lot of shame that comes with that.

709
00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:54.800
'cause you feel, I think what I started

710
00:28:55.630 --> 00:28:59.680
to really pick apart I guess I don't know

711
00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:02.490
if that's the right word, but dissect,

712
00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:06.120
was how is it possible

713
00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:12.760
to hold space for something like that?

714
00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:14.890
Like how is it possible to hold space for

715
00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:18.960
parts of you that you have a lot of shame in,

716
00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:22.290
parts of you that you have a lot of grief

717
00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:24.810
you grief parts of yourself and people pleasing,

718
00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:26.260
which is weird thing as well.

719
00:29:27.410 --> 00:29:29.650
And also not sound like a victim

720
00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:31.650
all the time or like you're complaining?

721
00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:34.120
so doing that works hard,

722
00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:36.060
'cause you don't know how to say it

723
00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:40.160
and feel like you can own it and claim it,

724
00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:41.130
'cause you don't wanna complain.

725
00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:43.182
You don't wanna sound like you're complaining to people.

726
00:29:43.182 --> 00:29:44.850
You don't want to be a burden to other people too,

727
00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:46.122
with that.

728
00:29:46.122 --> 00:29:46.998
So I just didn't,

729
00:29:46.998 --> 00:29:50.170
and I just didn't for however long and it manifested

730
00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:51.030
How did that work out?

731
00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:51.863
Horribly.

732
00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:55.313
All right.

733
00:29:55.313 --> 00:30:00.000
So by the time you're a senior in high school, though,

734
00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.700
you're crushing it, I assume you're getting recruited

735
00:30:02.700 --> 00:30:04.090
at all these colleges,

736
00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:06.983
you're probably rocking out the energy,

737
00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:10.630
records and all that kind of stuff at that point.

738
00:30:10.630 --> 00:30:15.630
Yeah, so my college recruiting experience

739
00:30:15.640 --> 00:30:17.730
was really interesting.

740
00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:22.350
I went to Arizona, Texas, Northwestern Florida,

741
00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:24.733
Cal and Stanford, Stanford.

742
00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:29.180
My mom was like, let's go to California.

743
00:30:29.180 --> 00:30:30.083
My dad's like no.

744
00:30:32.020 --> 00:30:35.540
So I ended up having to choose between Arizona

745
00:30:35.540 --> 00:30:37.780
and Florida 'cause I want to train with the guys.

746
00:30:37.780 --> 00:30:38.613
I love training

747
00:30:38.613 --> 00:30:39.446
with the guys.
Those were the only

748
00:30:39.446 --> 00:30:40.980
two programs where they train together?

749
00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:41.813
Yeah.

750
00:30:42.656 --> 00:30:44.780
I end up choosing--
So, what was that about?

751
00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:45.963
Why was that important?

752
00:30:47.170 --> 00:30:48.850
This is like kind of strange.

753
00:30:48.850 --> 00:30:53.300
So, I somehow knew that,

754
00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:55.594
first of all, I train with guys, I like side tons of guys,

755
00:30:55.594 --> 00:30:56.970
there was like five people

756
00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:58.670
in that senior team that were girls,

757
00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:00.500
the rest were dudes.

758
00:31:00.500 --> 00:31:03.610
My brothers, where I grew up around guys,

759
00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:04.690
like a lot of guy cousins.

760
00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:07.650
I just, I liked that energy for training

761
00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:09.400
I didn't even know what it was like

762
00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:11.533
to really train with all girls,

763
00:31:11.533 --> 00:31:12.830
so I was sort of just biased right off the start.

764
00:31:12.830 --> 00:31:15.320
But I also knew that the men back strokers

765
00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:17.960
in the 200, were equivalent

766
00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:19.930
to why I wanted to be in the 200 free.

767
00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:23.670
I didn't know times, but I remember my brother saying to me,

768
00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:26.550
if you wanna go really fast in you're 200 free,

769
00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:29.120
you need to train with dudes in the 200 back.

770
00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:30.810
'Cause it's the same time and I was like,

771
00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:31.850
whatever that means.

772
00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:34.540
But so, I checked the box of looking

773
00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:36.450
at University of Florida, palm trees

774
00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:38.540
like Mike De Boer best friend Martyn Wilby

775
00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:39.833
was one of the coaches.

776
00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:44.300
Men and women's team warm weather

777
00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:47.560
and a lot of male backstroker so I can train with them.

778
00:31:47.560 --> 00:31:48.393
Isn't that weird?

779
00:31:48.393 --> 00:31:50.630
And I ended up doing it my whole career.

780
00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:53.650
Right, well you're somebody who has a lot of guy friends

781
00:31:53.650 --> 00:31:56.900
it feels like most of your friends are dudes.

782
00:31:56.900 --> 00:31:59.790
So not, I think, the word masculine

783
00:31:59.790 --> 00:32:01.580
has a pejorative when used

784
00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:03.370
in association with somebody who's a woman,

785
00:32:03.370 --> 00:32:06.820
but there's like a, there is a masculine aspect

786
00:32:06.820 --> 00:32:09.652
to how you carry yourself socially.

787
00:32:09.652 --> 00:32:11.225
Yeah, I like to hang.

788
00:32:11.225 --> 00:32:13.280
Yeah, and you're super tight with your brother.

789
00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:16.727
Yeah and I really needed that experience.

790
00:32:16.727 --> 00:32:18.200
And I wanted that experience.

791
00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:20.193
And then he ended up going there.

792
00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:23.060
And that was like--
He transfer, though, right?

793
00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:25.480
Yeah, there's a lot that went on with that.

794
00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:28.740
Just a lot of yeah, there's a lot that went on with that.

795
00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:32.163
But Greg Troy, recruited me to University of Florida,

796
00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:38.010
and he was my main coach throughout the years there.

797
00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:42.360
And that, that's the whole up and down relationship

798
00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:43.260
between him and I.

799
00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:47.930
But yeah, so I went to University of Florida

800
00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:50.083
end up signing with them full scholarship.

801
00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:52.333
Went in my freshman year.

802
00:32:54.100 --> 00:32:57.290
Oh, man, I had the best freshman year,

803
00:32:57.290 --> 00:32:59.283
out of all the years really, actually.

804
00:33:01.104 --> 00:33:04.460
200 free 500 free at SECs, won them at SECs

805
00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:06.680
with second in both at NCAA's.

806
00:33:06.680 --> 00:33:08.250
And then my sophomore year came around,

807
00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:10.350
and I had so many expectations,

808
00:33:10.350 --> 00:33:11.520
and I just swam like crap.

809
00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:14.440
I mean, it was a rough year for me.

810
00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:15.990
So what was the change?

811
00:33:15.990 --> 00:33:18.053
Like, what do you attribute that dip to?

812
00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:22.097
Expectations so--

813
00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:26.350
So suddenly, you're this artistic personality

814
00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:28.510
who kind of just goes with the flow

815
00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:30.350
and suddenly you're in a position

816
00:33:30.350 --> 00:33:31.710
where you're actually paying attention

817
00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:33.130
to all that stuff that everybody

818
00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:34.460
had been telling you all along,

819
00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:36.437
that you needed to pay attention to

820
00:33:36.437 --> 00:33:38.983
and it had the opposite effect.

821
00:33:38.983 --> 00:33:39.816
Yeah, so all of a sudden,

822
00:33:39.816 --> 00:33:41.993
I had to pay attention to times like you said.

823
00:33:43.780 --> 00:33:45.840
And coming in as a freshman,

824
00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:48.810
there's no expectation, it's just, go.

825
00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:52.780
And Greg Tory put a lot of pressure on me my sophomore year,

826
00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:54.730
he also had me training in the distance group.

827
00:33:54.730 --> 00:33:58.660
So Anthony Nesty was my coach and Nesty is amazing.

828
00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:01.080
He's the greatest human.

829
00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:02.890
But every day I'd walk on the pool deck

830
00:34:02.890 --> 00:34:05.950
on the outdoor pool deck where the distance group trained,

831
00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:09.857
and he would say, "Why are you out here?

832
00:34:09.857 --> 00:34:11.410
"I don't know why you're out here."

833
00:34:11.410 --> 00:34:13.020
And I was like, I don't either.

834
00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:15.780
I don't, I cannot do four 1000s for time,

835
00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:17.670
that's not the way I train.

836
00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:22.010
So, I started doing this distance training

837
00:34:22.010 --> 00:34:23.800
and stepping out of the weight room

838
00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:26.210
and then into the pool more and training straight distance

839
00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:28.160
and my body didn't respond.

840
00:34:28.160 --> 00:34:32.930
I have pretty sensitive adrenals and so it was like, boom,

841
00:34:32.930 --> 00:34:37.910
I was, severely underweight, I wasn't training well,

842
00:34:37.910 --> 00:34:42.084
I wasn't competing well, I got sick a few times.

843
00:34:42.084 --> 00:34:43.610
And I was also in unhealthy relationship

844
00:34:43.610 --> 00:34:45.364
for the first year of my life

845
00:34:45.364 --> 00:34:48.350
and so it was just like, coming from every angle

846
00:34:48.350 --> 00:34:50.800
it was like the great storm of, oh, dear God,

847
00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:53.410
like now I have to prove myself

848
00:34:53.410 --> 00:34:54.880
and people don't like me again

849
00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:57.130
and I'm not well and I don't know what to do.

850
00:34:58.600 --> 00:35:00.400
Was there pressure to lose weight?

851
00:35:01.270 --> 00:35:02.103
Yeah.

852
00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:05.040
Every day I went on the pool deck,

853
00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:07.990
I was told, either tighten it up

854
00:35:07.990 --> 00:35:10.073
or go on an extra run this week or,

855
00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:15.380
there were specific groups for extra runs and stuff,

856
00:35:15.380 --> 00:35:20.380
and I was not anywhere near needing to do an extra run.

857
00:35:22.858 --> 00:35:27.858
And that was a really, really hard time for me.

858
00:35:30.088 --> 00:35:31.810
I didn't know either how to,

859
00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:33.560
I didn't have a voice, I didn't speak up,

860
00:35:33.560 --> 00:35:34.833
I didn't say a damn word.

861
00:35:36.168 --> 00:35:37.920
'Cause I believed in what was going on.

862
00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:38.993
I believed in it.

863
00:35:40.130 --> 00:35:43.310
And I just remember being so confused.

864
00:35:43.310 --> 00:35:46.240
Like, I don't understand why I'm being told these things

865
00:35:46.240 --> 00:35:47.670
why am I not enough?

866
00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:49.550
So we're back to that again.

867
00:35:49.550 --> 00:35:52.050
Yeah, well, you have a certain level of success,

868
00:35:53.157 --> 00:35:55.360
you're this big fish in a small pond in Kentucky,

869
00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:58.410
but then, for people that are unfamiliar,

870
00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:01.519
like the University of Florida was a mecca of swimming.

871
00:36:01.519 --> 00:36:03.600
You were in that bubble pool, right?

872
00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:05.230
Yeah.
That epic

873
00:36:05.230 --> 00:36:06.860
architectural pool that.

874
00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:07.933
Is that still there?

875
00:36:08.770 --> 00:36:09.603
I love that pool.

876
00:36:09.603 --> 00:36:12.030
There is a little five lane outdoor pool,

877
00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:15.010
Right, Greg Troy legendary coach

878
00:36:15.010 --> 00:36:18.070
Anthony Nesty Olympic gold medalist in 100 fly

879
00:36:18.070 --> 00:36:19.593
from Suriname.

880
00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:22.910
I remember they when he won the gold medal,

881
00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:24.720
they like they put him on the currency.

882
00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:26.260
Like he was such a national hero.

883
00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:28.400
Oh, yeah, he would go back for parades

884
00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:32.620
and they parade him around in a chair the whole deal.

885
00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:36.670
And he married this beautiful, tall, redhead woman

886
00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.920
and they had the most amazing little girl.

887
00:36:38.920 --> 00:36:41.140
I mean, he was just fantastic.

888
00:36:41.140 --> 00:36:44.330
And honestly, unfortunately,

889
00:36:44.330 --> 00:36:46.690
he was the distance coach didn't have a lot of interaction

890
00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:47.840
with him after sophomore year.

891
00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:49.790
What was the decision that you needed to go

892
00:36:49.790 --> 00:36:50.910
into the distance program?

893
00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:52.870
because I mean, that ends up being your thing,

894
00:36:52.870 --> 00:36:55.980
but that wasn't what you arrived thinking

895
00:36:55.980 --> 00:36:57.180
you were gonna be doing.

896
00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:00.520
He wanted me to swim the mile.

897
00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:03.751
He wanted me to --
Because?

898
00:37:03.751 --> 00:37:04.584
I don't know.

899
00:37:04.584 --> 00:37:06.160
I really don't know.

900
00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:07.660
Well, he's kinda right.

901
00:37:07.660 --> 00:37:10.240
I mean, I was good at the 500.

902
00:37:10.240 --> 00:37:12.480
That was the--
800 meters still.

903
00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:15.410
Yeah, but I wasn't mentally a miler.

904
00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:17.070
That wasn't my thing.

905
00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:20.100
I was a very much 500 that went

906
00:37:20.100 --> 00:37:22.653
toward the 200 and not the other way.

907
00:37:25.410 --> 00:37:29.393
But I mean, the amount we trained,

908
00:37:31.319 --> 00:37:34.130
John's interview with you, oh, my God,

909
00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:37.470
I mean, we were training four times a day.

910
00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:38.360
Four times a day?

911
00:37:38.360 --> 00:37:40.130
Yeah, we had morning practice,

912
00:37:40.130 --> 00:37:42.540
we had weights and dry land,

913
00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:43.760
and we had afternoon practice.

914
00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:46.380
So morning, go to class, go to weights,

915
00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:48.930
go back to dry land to go to afternoon practice.

916
00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:51.430
Eat go to bed.
So two full sessions

917
00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:53.483
and two gym sessions every day.

918
00:37:55.234 --> 00:37:56.220
And I was tapped.
And you're looking

919
00:37:57.240 --> 00:38:01.500
maybe 15,000 yards a day or something.

920
00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:03.650
Some days, way more.

921
00:38:03.650 --> 00:38:08.650
But I was just tapped, and so I think there was a belief

922
00:38:09.160 --> 00:38:11.090
that I needed more training.

923
00:38:11.090 --> 00:38:12.850
And I remember one day,

924
00:38:12.850 --> 00:38:15.440
I walked into the pool deck sophomore year

925
00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:20.440
and it was like, four 2000, no nine 1650s.

926
00:38:22.790 --> 00:38:25.740
And yeah, I mean, I'm not kidding, Rich

927
00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:28.470
I start crying, immediately start like,

928
00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:30.780
and I was I didn't know what to do.

929
00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:32.630
1650 is a mile.

930
00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:36.189
So nine, nine one mile repeats in the pool.

931
00:38:36.189 --> 00:38:37.022
I mean, let's put it this way.

932
00:38:37.022 --> 00:38:38.730
It takes from two until like seven.

933
00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:40.770
So I just start crying.

934
00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:43.633
Unlike, the 18 minutes or something like that.

935
00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:46.490
And so Nesty comes up to me,

936
00:38:46.490 --> 00:38:48.807
he puts his hands on my shoulders and he was like,

937
00:38:48.807 --> 00:38:50.237
"You're gonna be okay."

938
00:38:51.404 --> 00:38:54.163
I don't know if I am actually, lemme give this a try.

939
00:38:55.671 --> 00:38:56.504
But they pulled me out at four.

940
00:38:56.504 --> 00:38:59.248
So that day only did four but I'll never forget,

941
00:38:59.248 --> 00:39:02.928
I got out at four and they still had five more it

942
00:39:02.928 --> 00:39:06.860
they didn't finish like seven by the way 07:00 p.m.

943
00:39:06.860 --> 00:39:08.520
And I called Mike de Boer.

944
00:39:08.520 --> 00:39:10.760
And I'm sitting on the side of the pool outdoor pool,

945
00:39:10.760 --> 00:39:13.820
like over in a corner on my little flip cell phone

946
00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:16.433
it's like 2005 and a half or whatever.

947
00:39:18.427 --> 00:39:20.670
And I just said something's not right.

948
00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:23.520
I'm not doing what I need to be doing here,

949
00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:25.184
something isn't right.

950
00:39:25.184 --> 00:39:27.703
And he was just, he talked me through it.

951
00:39:29.210 --> 00:39:30.043
What are you training?

952
00:39:30.043 --> 00:39:30.876
What's going on?

953
00:39:30.876 --> 00:39:31.709
Whatever.

954
00:39:31.709 --> 00:39:33.100
I guess what happened was he ended up talking

955
00:39:33.100 --> 00:39:35.450
to one of the coaches Wilby.

956
00:39:35.450 --> 00:39:37.230
Wilby talked to Troy and it was like,

957
00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:39.410
we need to switch around her training again.

958
00:39:39.410 --> 00:39:41.190
Like she can't be training this way

959
00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:42.660
it's not working for her.

960
00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:44.891
So they ended up putting me back

961
00:39:44.891 --> 00:39:46.231
in the weight room two extra days a week

962
00:39:46.231 --> 00:39:47.064
and putting me in the middle distance group

963
00:39:47.064 --> 00:39:49.230
and breaststroke group, brushwork I am

964
00:39:49.230 --> 00:39:52.733
and I started swimming way better with the lights out.

965
00:39:54.700 --> 00:39:57.380
So, the balance of that but also I think

966
00:39:57.380 --> 00:39:59.610
as I sort of have reflected on that

967
00:39:59.610 --> 00:40:01.710
feeling seen from Mike

968
00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:05.540
and then having a conversation with my coach

969
00:40:05.540 --> 00:40:07.220
and connecting on that level,

970
00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:09.000
and having a real conversation

971
00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:10.930
about what's happening with my body,

972
00:40:10.930 --> 00:40:15.160
why my cycles are gone, why I'm sick all the time

973
00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:16.470
why my blood work sucks,

974
00:40:16.470 --> 00:40:19.490
like getting real with the actual biological effects

975
00:40:19.490 --> 00:40:22.890
of what's gone on inside my body without over training

976
00:40:22.890 --> 00:40:27.010
was eye opening for them and also, for me.

977
00:40:27.010 --> 00:40:28.350
I've never been over trained before,

978
00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:30.950
really like I didn't understand what that felt like.

979
00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:36.730
So that really was a the first time in my life,

980
00:40:36.730 --> 00:40:41.090
I saw the combination of the mind body working together

981
00:40:41.090 --> 00:40:45.370
to kind of pivot me back on course.

982
00:40:45.370 --> 00:40:47.230
I didn't have that connection before,

983
00:40:47.230 --> 00:40:49.760
I always, so to kind of refer back

984
00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:51.830
to what I was saying earlier.

985
00:40:51.830 --> 00:40:55.750
I always sort of felt weird, 'cause I had that innately.

986
00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:58.190
I knew that I was a semantic experiencer

987
00:40:58.190 --> 00:41:00.269
and I felt things in my body

988
00:41:00.269 --> 00:41:01.380
but I didn't understand that the brain

989
00:41:01.380 --> 00:41:03.227
and the body were working together to feel things

990
00:41:03.227 --> 00:41:05.577
and that where you feel one you feel the other.

991
00:41:06.500 --> 00:41:10.320
It's very similar to John Moffat's trajectory.

992
00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:12.960
'Cause he was, a guy who couldn't handle

993
00:41:12.960 --> 00:41:16.380
that kind of volume, but had the self awareness

994
00:41:17.438 --> 00:41:19.140
and the boundaries, the healthy boundaries

995
00:41:19.140 --> 00:41:21.580
to say I'm not going to that morning workout,

996
00:41:21.580 --> 00:41:24.770
it's not in service to the goals that I've set

997
00:41:24.770 --> 00:41:26.020
or of the team.

998
00:41:26.020 --> 00:41:29.010
And I think people don't understand how difficult

999
00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:31.030
it is to create that boundary

1000
00:41:31.030 --> 00:41:33.880
because you're a part of this program that's legendary

1001
00:41:33.880 --> 00:41:35.330
and this is what we do here.

1002
00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:38.500
So get in the pool and don't ask questions.

1003
00:41:38.500 --> 00:41:40.600
I used to have my academic advisor, Tim,

1004
00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:43.641
pull me out of workouts,

1005
00:41:43.641 --> 00:41:45.990
'cause he knew how drained and tired I was,

1006
00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:48.000
my grades were suffering.

1007
00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:52.220
And he would just have me sit in the OSL,

1008
00:41:52.220 --> 00:41:54.890
the Office of Student Life over in the corner

1009
00:41:54.890 --> 00:41:58.050
and tell coach that I had a tutor, quote/unquote.

1010
00:41:58.050 --> 00:42:00.880
'Cause I was so drained, yeah, I would just sleep.

1011
00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:03.390
And it takes a long time to emerge out of that.

1012
00:42:03.390 --> 00:42:05.010
Super long, I felt very guilty

1013
00:42:05.010 --> 00:42:06.090
held a lot of guilt there,

1014
00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:08.100
'cause it felt like everyone else's training,

1015
00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:09.680
but I was also going through trauma,

1016
00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:13.160
like I was also going through a very unhealthy situation

1017
00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:15.150
with a relationship and so not only that,

1018
00:42:15.150 --> 00:42:17.550
my whole systems and sympathetic 24 seven,

1019
00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:21.690
showing up to the pool, with zero hours of sleep,

1020
00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:23.270
'cause I'm up all night before

1021
00:42:23.270 --> 00:42:24.640
going through whatever I'm going through,

1022
00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:26.000
and I get to the that

1023
00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:27.610
I wasn't even accounting for that.

1024
00:42:27.610 --> 00:42:29.240
I didn't realize how much that

1025
00:42:29.240 --> 00:42:32.130
was playing a part in my performance.

1026
00:42:32.130 --> 00:42:34.770
And it was like, eye opening to me

1027
00:42:34.770 --> 00:42:38.870
to finally realize that that was working against me.

1028
00:42:38.870 --> 00:42:40.760
All of these factors were working against me

1029
00:42:40.760 --> 00:42:43.840
and if I didn't align myself in the right path, mind

1030
00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:46.420
and body, I wasn't gonna go anywhere.

1031
00:42:46.420 --> 00:42:47.870
But you emerge out of that.

1032
00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:49.543
Yeah.

1033
00:42:51.350 --> 00:42:54.390
Senior year, NC Double A's

1034
00:42:54.390 --> 00:42:57.910
was like the big emergence out of it, I slowly emerged.

1035
00:42:57.910 --> 00:43:01.080
But that was my, probably my favorite part.

1036
00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:03.370
Was that 2008?

1037
00:43:03.370 --> 00:43:08.370
So 2008, you break a (indistinct) record in the 500 Free?

1038
00:43:09.587 --> 00:43:12.350
And that was the longest standing record at the time,

1039
00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:13.630
right it was a Janet Evans?

1040
00:43:13.630 --> 00:43:14.463
Yeah.

1041
00:43:14.463 --> 00:43:15.960
I mean, that's pretty epic.

1042
00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:18.629
That was my favorite performance of my life.

1043
00:43:18.629 --> 00:43:20.230
It was like 433, or something like that?

1044
00:43:20.230 --> 00:43:24.390
Yeah, and I ripped two suits before that

1045
00:43:24.390 --> 00:43:27.280
and I remember just being totally in flow, like,

1046
00:43:27.280 --> 00:43:29.173
I don't remember that race at all.

1047
00:43:31.567 --> 00:43:32.940
I touched the wall, and I had oodles of energy,

1048
00:43:32.940 --> 00:43:35.240
I could have gone for another 500.

1049
00:43:35.240 --> 00:43:37.943
And I remember just being like, holy shit,

