1229
00:52:30.090 --> 00:52:35.090
It's definitely, it really challenges, your focus,

1230
00:52:36.250 --> 00:52:38.870
it challenges your ability to stay in your lane,

1231
00:52:38.870 --> 00:52:42.520
and to be able to block things out really well.

1232
00:52:42.520 --> 00:52:45.610
Especially with media and with with other things going on.

1233
00:52:45.610 --> 00:52:47.760
Luckily, then we didn't have a lot of media.

1234
00:52:47.760 --> 00:52:50.010
There wasn't we didn't have phones at the Olympics.

1235
00:52:50.010 --> 00:52:53.090
We didn't have phones.
Facebook was online,

1236
00:52:53.090 --> 00:52:56.180
but it wasn't there was no iPhone or anything like that.

1237
00:52:56.180 --> 00:52:58.843
No social media, no iPhones,

1238
00:52:58.843 --> 00:53:00.860
we didn't have any technology in the village.

1239
00:53:00.860 --> 00:53:04.100
The village is a very, dense experience,

1240
00:53:04.100 --> 00:53:06.420
there's just a lot of people obviously vying

1241
00:53:06.420 --> 00:53:08.450
for the same thing. so the energy is just palpable

1242
00:53:08.450 --> 00:53:10.140
you can feel it from everybody.

1243
00:53:10.140 --> 00:53:13.340
But there's also this camaraderie, it's beautiful.

1244
00:53:13.340 --> 00:53:16.380
You see every country and every walk of life,

1245
00:53:16.380 --> 00:53:19.550
and it's a really special experience.

1246
00:53:19.550 --> 00:53:21.870
And you see a lot that you've never seen before.

1247
00:53:21.870 --> 00:53:24.496
As girl from Kentucky and Florida.

1248
00:53:24.496 --> 00:53:25.830
And I mean, I'm seeing the world, right,

1249
00:53:25.830 --> 00:53:27.280
you're seeing the world right in front of you

1250
00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:29.040
and you have to really check yourself

1251
00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:32.320
with what you've, been thinking your whole life

1252
00:53:32.320 --> 00:53:35.862
and put your judgment aside and be able to hone in

1253
00:53:35.862 --> 00:53:38.460
on really what you're doing accept everybody,

1254
00:53:38.460 --> 00:53:39.660
and kind of move forward.

1255
00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:41.630
That's the point of the Olympics, right?

1256
00:53:41.630 --> 00:53:43.290
Is to accept everybody.

1257
00:53:43.290 --> 00:53:46.503
The rings symbolize that unity of the world together.

1258
00:53:48.180 --> 00:53:49.510
So it is a special experience ,

1259
00:53:49.510 --> 00:53:50.930
where you're trying to compete against each other,

1260
00:53:50.930 --> 00:53:53.867
but you're really trying to bring everybody together

1261
00:53:53.867 --> 00:53:57.840
of the world into this special place to achieve this goal.

1262
00:53:57.840 --> 00:54:00.740
Do you feel like in the wake of

1263
00:54:00.740 --> 00:54:05.740
that you you departed Beijing thinking, I'm just starting,

1264
00:54:05.820 --> 00:54:08.000
I got a bunch of force and I was on this relay

1265
00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:10.700
and I got a medal but my individual time

1266
00:54:10.700 --> 00:54:14.210
would have meddled in this event, I'm getting back to work?

1267
00:54:14.210 --> 00:54:15.043
No.

1268
00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:22.393
I knew I was bummed that I couldn't get meddled on my own,

1269
00:54:23.770 --> 00:54:27.360
but I was so burned out at that point,

1270
00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:32.360
I was beyond ready to walk away and to run away.

1271
00:54:37.270 --> 00:54:40.010
I was listening to Apollo's podcast with you

1272
00:54:40.010 --> 00:54:44.170
and he just, some of the things he said struck a chord too,

1273
00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:47.290
just with, the weight of gold and how that all works.

1274
00:54:47.290 --> 00:54:48.980
And we can go down that path, but,

1275
00:54:48.980 --> 00:54:51.997
it's a whole experience of what now?

1276
00:55:00.321 --> 00:55:02.530
And that's the easy way to say it.

1277
00:55:02.530 --> 00:55:04.730
But there really is no better way to say it.

1278
00:55:06.070 --> 00:55:10.500
It's daunting, and also, you can't be bothered

1279
00:55:10.500 --> 00:55:12.767
to go back into the fires.

1280
00:55:12.767 --> 00:55:16.180
And they are like, we're so good as athletes at focusing,

1281
00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:18.650
and at pivoting our focus.

1282
00:55:18.650 --> 00:55:23.120
So we pivot to running, and you don't look back that way,

1283
00:55:23.120 --> 00:55:24.680
'cause it really damn good at what you do.

1284
00:55:24.680 --> 00:55:28.071
So you're gonna focus this way instead of focusing.

1285
00:55:28.071 --> 00:55:30.110
But you don't know what to focus that spotlight on,

1286
00:55:30.110 --> 00:55:31.809
that's the problem.

1287
00:55:31.809 --> 00:55:36.080
And on paper, like from a academic perspective,

1288
00:55:36.080 --> 00:55:38.520
who wouldn't wanna hire an Olympian,

1289
00:55:38.520 --> 00:55:40.010
these people know how to work hard,

1290
00:55:40.010 --> 00:55:41.000
they know how to set a goal,

1291
00:55:41.000 --> 00:55:42.030
they know how to achieve it,

1292
00:55:42.030 --> 00:55:44.090
they know how to show up under pressure

1293
00:55:44.090 --> 00:55:46.220
and overcome obstacles.

1294
00:55:46.220 --> 00:55:48.690
They just seem like the ultimate candidate

1295
00:55:48.690 --> 00:55:53.130
for the marketplace, but it doesn't work that way.

1296
00:55:53.130 --> 00:55:57.490
And I think the more often than not,

1297
00:55:57.490 --> 00:56:00.160
it is the weight of gold, where they become,

1298
00:56:00.160 --> 00:56:01.880
they have this existential crisis,

1299
00:56:01.880 --> 00:56:03.180
like, what am I supposed to do now?

1300
00:56:03.180 --> 00:56:05.010
I've never thought about this,

1301
00:56:05.010 --> 00:56:08.110
because getting to where they got

1302
00:56:08.110 --> 00:56:10.454
to achieve what they achieved required every ounce

1303
00:56:10.454 --> 00:56:14.540
of focus and discipline and intention.

1304
00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:18.470
There just wasn't any time to ponder what comes after.

1305
00:56:18.470 --> 00:56:21.010
And there aren't structures in place

1306
00:56:21.010 --> 00:56:24.630
to help athletes with that transition.

1307
00:56:24.630 --> 00:56:25.870
So, that's why I think that movie

1308
00:56:25.870 --> 00:56:29.530
was so powerful and instructive.

1309
00:56:29.530 --> 00:56:30.740
It was incredible.

1310
00:56:30.740 --> 00:56:33.740
I watched it a few times, and was just sobbing my eyes down.

1311
00:56:35.739 --> 00:56:39.330
Because, again, it goes back to that same conversation

1312
00:56:39.330 --> 00:56:42.660
of how can you be feeling two things at once,

1313
00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:46.070
so proud, like, sick, Lolo Jones, like sick,

1314
00:56:46.070 --> 00:56:48.290
I just want this medal, and also, I'm making smoothies,

1315
00:56:48.290 --> 00:56:51.370
So it's like, how can you be so angry,

1316
00:56:51.370 --> 00:56:55.820
and harbor this anger and frustration at what you've done?

1317
00:56:55.820 --> 00:56:59.810
And also be so proud and so excited

1318
00:56:59.810 --> 00:57:01.070
that you've done this thing

1319
00:57:01.070 --> 00:57:03.310
and can't wait to like, tell your friends?

1320
00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:04.643
Like, even though they know.

1321
00:57:04.643 --> 00:57:06.210
I can't wait to do all these other things.

1322
00:57:06.210 --> 00:57:09.610
And--
You can't tell your friends

1323
00:57:09.610 --> 00:57:10.750
that you feel that way?

1324
00:57:10.750 --> 00:57:13.480
No, so much shame with that.

1325
00:57:13.480 --> 00:57:16.719
Because they would think you're an entitled spoiled brat.

1326
00:57:16.719 --> 00:57:17.990
Or, that was my story.

1327
00:57:17.990 --> 00:57:19.787
No one wants to hear that sob story,

1328
00:57:19.787 --> 00:57:21.560
and Michael Phelps says it really well, too,

1329
00:57:21.560 --> 00:57:23.070
he says the same thing.

1330
00:57:23.070 --> 00:57:25.490
I couldn't possibly tell people I was depressed,

1331
00:57:25.490 --> 00:57:27.900
I had 700 gold medals.

1332
00:57:27.900 --> 00:57:30.800
There's no way I could tell somebody that I'm depressed.

1333
00:57:30.800 --> 00:57:32.573
There's no way that I could share,

1334
00:57:34.780 --> 00:57:36.130
that I've had suicidal thoughts.

1335
00:57:36.130 --> 00:57:38.100
There's no way that I could share that

1336
00:57:39.310 --> 00:57:42.040
I was in a traumatic situation or relationship.

1337
00:57:42.040 --> 00:57:43.990
There's no way that I could share that,

1338
00:57:46.630 --> 00:57:51.630
God, that I hated myself at a certain part of,

1339
00:57:52.960 --> 00:57:54.110
day four at the Olympics.

1340
00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:57.660
There's no way you can share these specific feelings.

1341
00:57:57.660 --> 00:58:00.420
Because what, then?

1342
00:58:00.420 --> 00:58:02.400
what would what would people think of you?

1343
00:58:02.400 --> 00:58:06.440
Right, you're you're a bulletproof Olympian?

1344
00:58:06.440 --> 00:58:08.030
Yeah, you gotta be Superman.

1345
00:58:08.030 --> 00:58:12.930
You've gotta be this special force

1346
00:58:12.930 --> 00:58:16.510
that, people would kill to be in your shoes.

1347
00:58:16.510 --> 00:58:21.510
And so yeah, you could never say something

1348
00:58:21.950 --> 00:58:26.950
that would jeopardize your Olympic status.

1349
00:58:28.490 --> 00:58:32.260
Because then if people find out

1350
00:58:32.260 --> 00:58:34.110
and not only people, but media,

1351
00:58:34.110 --> 00:58:36.210
let's say media finds out there,

1352
00:58:36.210 --> 00:58:38.759
oh, she's complaining about--

1353
00:58:38.759 --> 00:58:40.442
She's got a bad attitude.

1354
00:58:40.442 --> 00:58:41.275
Yeah, she's got a bad attitude.

1355
00:58:41.275 --> 00:58:42.620
So there's--
She's a bad Olympian.

1356
00:58:42.620 --> 00:58:47.620
Right. and the, the psychology of it

1357
00:58:47.630 --> 00:58:50.190
is changing, because I think there's a lot

1358
00:58:50.190 --> 00:58:51.650
of awareness now around the fact

1359
00:58:51.650 --> 00:58:56.170
that you can be depressed or having a difficult time

1360
00:58:56.170 --> 00:58:57.560
or going through something traumatic

1361
00:58:57.560 --> 00:58:58.623
and also perform.

1362
00:58:59.720 --> 00:59:00.970
That both of those are okay.

1363
00:59:00.970 --> 00:59:02.750
Again, back to the two things.

1364
00:59:02.750 --> 00:59:06.250
Yeah, well I mean, I think, Michael is to be credited,

1365
00:59:06.250 --> 00:59:08.120
considerably for the work that he's done

1366
00:59:08.120 --> 00:59:09.840
to expand that awareness.

1367
00:59:09.840 --> 00:59:10.893
Huge.

1368
00:59:11.936 --> 00:59:13.587
And--
I mean,

1369
00:59:13.587 --> 00:59:14.740
were you were on the Olympic team with him,

1370
00:59:14.740 --> 00:59:17.548
is there, you've spent a lot of time with him, I presume.

1371
00:59:17.548 --> 00:59:18.687
Yeah, he's great .

1372
00:59:20.770 --> 00:59:22.350
Was there any into, like,

1373
00:59:22.350 --> 00:59:25.230
I'm sure your your, your relationship with him

1374
00:59:25.230 --> 00:59:27.190
is when you're at swim Meets.

1375
00:59:27.190 --> 00:59:28.870
So could see that aspect of it?

1376
00:59:28.870 --> 00:59:31.640
Yeah know, and we saw it was Michael though,

1377
00:59:31.640 --> 00:59:33.163
just because he's,

1378
00:59:34.160 --> 00:59:37.770
he's vulnerable, he could show he wears his emotions,

1379
00:59:37.770 --> 00:59:40.700
on a sleeve and so you could see him struggling,

1380
00:59:40.700 --> 00:59:43.040
but you don't know to what extent.

1381
00:59:43.040 --> 00:59:44.340
You don't know if that's just,

1382
00:59:44.340 --> 00:59:46.830
at that point time we're also laser focused.

1383
00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:49.670
You don't know if that's just with training

1384
00:59:49.670 --> 00:59:52.550
or with not going times you wanna go,

1385
00:59:52.550 --> 00:59:55.510
whatever it is, you just assume that

1386
00:59:55.510 --> 00:59:58.510
that person is struggling for another reason.

1387
00:59:58.510 --> 01:00:03.190
Not with worth, even though I was struggling with worth.

1388
01:00:03.190 --> 01:00:05.030
So, it's like you can feel it,

1389
01:00:05.030 --> 01:00:07.323
but we just never It was like hushed,

1390
01:00:07.323 --> 01:00:09.410
but no one would ever utter those words.

1391
01:00:09.410 --> 01:00:11.790
We all feel this way, but no one's saying anything.

1392
01:00:11.790 --> 01:00:13.750
So he was like the cats out of the bag,

1393
01:00:13.750 --> 01:00:15.800
he broke the mold there have.

1394
01:00:15.800 --> 01:00:17.267
We've all been feeling this,

1395
01:00:17.267 --> 01:00:19.680
and somebody just needs to say it

1396
01:00:19.680 --> 01:00:22.405
'cause we're all feeling it and walking around

1397
01:00:22.405 --> 01:00:23.910
with this like weird thing where everyone's awkward

1398
01:00:25.280 --> 01:00:26.181
and Oh yeah, I'm great, how are you?

1399
01:00:26.181 --> 01:00:28.087
I'm great, years later, and that's not the case.

1400
01:00:28.087 --> 01:00:30.230
Like, let's just say it how it is

1401
01:00:30.230 --> 01:00:32.490
and also you can be struggling and be proud

1402
01:00:32.490 --> 01:00:33.900
of your experience at the same time.

1403
01:00:33.900 --> 01:00:36.000
No one's telling you, you can't have both.

1404
01:00:36.990 --> 01:00:38.430
And I think that a lot of us felt like

1405
01:00:38.430 --> 01:00:40.190
we couldn't have both.

1406
01:00:40.190 --> 01:00:42.213
So you kinda cut and run, right?

1407
01:00:42.213 --> 01:00:44.090
Like you just you just walk away from the pool

1408
01:00:44.090 --> 01:00:47.700
for a little while, I mean, you found your way back to it,

1409
01:00:47.700 --> 01:00:50.230
but you took this break.

1410
01:00:50.230 --> 01:00:52.500
So I cut and run.

1411
01:00:52.500 --> 01:00:57.050
I didn't look one direction from a pool for years.

1412
01:00:57.050 --> 01:00:58.400
First time I stepped inside

1413
01:00:59.893 --> 01:01:01.844
of a pool.
Years.

1414
01:01:01.844 --> 01:01:02.677
Years, probably two and a half.

1415
01:01:03.865 --> 01:01:05.090
I retired in 2010.

1416
01:01:05.090 --> 01:01:07.640
First time I stepped foot back

1417
01:01:07.640 --> 01:01:10.400
into a pool was Clark's Olympic trials.

1418
01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:13.493
And that was like, really hard for me.

1419
01:01:15.700 --> 01:01:18.150
I cried so much at that Meet by myself

1420
01:01:18.150 --> 01:01:19.530
in the hotel room in Omaha.

1421
01:01:19.530 --> 01:01:22.760
And I was so proud of my brother,

1422
01:01:22.760 --> 01:01:24.530
I have never seen a better race

1423
01:01:24.530 --> 01:01:26.620
than his race to make the Olympic team

1424
01:01:26.620 --> 01:01:29.690
in that 200 breaststroke is hands down

1425
01:01:29.690 --> 01:01:32.170
the best underwater pullout kid in the world.

1426
01:01:32.170 --> 01:01:34.940
I mean, he's insane, he's so strong.

1427
01:01:34.940 --> 01:01:36.620
And I was so proud of him

1428
01:01:36.620 --> 01:01:38.963
and so emotional there at that Meet.

1429
01:01:40.590 --> 01:01:42.630
Of course, I didn't show anybody I cried my hotel room.

1430
01:01:42.630 --> 01:01:46.360
'Cause of course--
Brother, sister duo's,

1431
01:01:46.360 --> 01:01:49.653
that have both become Olympians, there must be?

1432
01:01:50.600 --> 01:01:52.393
Crippen's, I believe.

1433
01:01:54.350 --> 01:01:55.620
Or no, maybe I'm thinking of something.

1434
01:01:55.620 --> 01:01:56.987
I don't know.
I don't think so.

1435
01:01:56.987 --> 01:01:59.350
Maybe another sports.

1436
01:01:59.350 --> 01:02:04.350
In swimming, there's sisters like Haley and Alyssa Anderson.

1437
01:02:08.954 --> 01:02:11.380
No, but that was, that was the first time

1438
01:02:11.380 --> 01:02:14.020
so you can imagine I ran away and the first time I come back

1439
01:02:14.020 --> 01:02:16.260
is around the exact same world,

1440
01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:17.920
the world I ran away from it.

1441
01:02:17.920 --> 01:02:20.630
And I had a purpose.

1442
01:02:20.630 --> 01:02:22.780
My purpose was to support my brother.

1443
01:02:22.780 --> 01:02:25.890
So I was able to again, block out my stuff,

1444
01:02:25.890 --> 01:02:28.110
handle it on my own and be there for him.

1445
01:02:28.110 --> 01:02:29.810
And I traded that whole Olympic trials,

1446
01:02:29.810 --> 01:02:32.550
like I was competing, my ass was up at 04:00 a.m

1447
01:02:32.550 --> 01:02:34.870
going on a run, getting ready eating breakfast,

1448
01:02:34.870 --> 01:02:38.080
like the whole, like, I felt like I was in the experience,

1449
01:02:38.080 --> 01:02:42.293
but I was not experience like I wasn't in that experience.

1450
01:02:43.570 --> 01:02:46.630
But so he makes the team and then we go to London.

1451
01:02:46.630 --> 01:02:49.780
And that again, it's like my second Meet back.

1452
01:02:49.780 --> 01:02:51.660
And that was really difficult for me.

1453
01:02:51.660 --> 01:02:53.443
So throughout this whole process,

1454
01:02:54.730 --> 01:02:55.700
difficult but proud.

1455
01:02:55.700 --> 01:02:57.280
So I mean, I can't tell you,

1456
01:02:57.280 --> 01:02:59.840
I was way more proud of my brother than I ever was myself.

1457
01:02:59.840 --> 01:03:01.140
It's crazy how that works.

1458
01:03:02.070 --> 01:03:06.890
And I get to oh, well throughout that process,

1459
01:03:06.890 --> 01:03:11.890
I was like struggling with some body dysmorphia,

1460
01:03:12.740 --> 01:03:16.360
some disordered eating, wasn't healthy,

1461
01:03:16.360 --> 01:03:17.460
let's put it that way,

1462
01:03:18.870 --> 01:03:22.088
at all, like just full stop, wasn't healthy,

1463
01:03:22.088 --> 01:03:25.750
wasn't eating wasn't taking care of myself,

1464
01:03:25.750 --> 01:03:28.053
didn't feel worthy of love, period.

1465
01:03:29.100 --> 01:03:31.740
Didn't have that my system had been in overdrive

1466
01:03:33.230 --> 01:03:37.360
and had been in sympathetic literally, for years.

1467
01:03:37.360 --> 01:03:39.260
And I had not come down.

1468
01:03:39.260 --> 01:03:43.580
My body was churning every last morsel of energy

1469
01:03:43.580 --> 01:03:45.260
just to get through that Meet.

1470
01:03:45.260 --> 01:03:48.273
And I got back from that Meet and I just like,

1471
01:03:49.270 --> 01:03:52.600
crashed, like my sit, my body just started to break down

1472
01:03:52.600 --> 01:03:55.670
it was this whole thing with that.

1473
01:03:55.670 --> 01:03:59.203
But that was the that was sort of the first realization

1474
01:03:59.203 --> 01:04:01.700
that I had that I wasn't healthy.

1475
01:04:01.700 --> 01:04:04.840
So between 2008 the Olympics,

1476
01:04:04.840 --> 01:04:07.450
you walking away from the sport and 2012

1477
01:04:07.450 --> 01:04:10.050
when you you don't really return to swimming,

1478
01:04:10.050 --> 01:04:13.040
but you return to the culture,

1479
01:04:13.040 --> 01:04:16.933
to support your brother a lot, there's a lot that happened.

1480
01:04:16.933 --> 01:04:19.570
So I wanna spend a little bit of time here.

1481
01:04:19.570 --> 01:04:20.950
I mean, you end up,

1482
01:04:20.950 --> 01:04:24.350
you're trying to find your way you get a fashion degree,

1483
01:04:24.350 --> 01:04:25.470
you got to fit them, right,

1484
01:04:25.470 --> 01:04:27.660
and you're working with some apparel brands

1485
01:04:27.660 --> 01:04:30.653
and you're employed, you're tryna figure it out.

1486
01:04:31.890 --> 01:04:34.650
But I feel like there's a crucial event

1487
01:04:34.650 --> 01:04:36.473
that takes place that I think,

1488
01:04:38.730 --> 01:04:43.130
puts you deeper into that psychological hole

1489
01:04:43.130 --> 01:04:45.040
and that happens around 2010.

1490
01:04:45.040 --> 01:04:46.650
So can we talk about that a little bit?

1491
01:04:46.650 --> 01:04:51.650
Yeah, so I had transferred from

1492
01:04:51.810 --> 01:04:54.620
the FIDM OC campus to the LA campus.

1493
01:04:54.620 --> 01:04:56.370
So I was living with Haley Piersol.

1494
01:04:58.500 --> 01:05:01.800
And Aaron came to visit for three months.

1495
01:05:01.800 --> 01:05:02.873
Classic Aaron move.

1496
01:05:04.599 --> 01:05:06.300
Is he's like living in Costa Rica now or something.

1497
01:05:06.300 --> 01:05:07.423
I guess, yeah.

1498
01:05:08.270 --> 01:05:09.900
But he came to live with us.

1499
01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:11.530
He just lived with us, I was on the corner

1500
01:05:11.530 --> 01:05:13.460
of La Cienega and again in Olympic.

1501
01:05:13.460 --> 01:05:14.480
What am I doing here?

1502
01:05:14.480 --> 01:05:17.500
Right in LA?
Oh my God.

1503
01:05:17.500 --> 01:05:21.623
And I was working for Lululemon.

1504
01:05:23.926 --> 01:05:26.263
I just like, I didn't know what to do.

1505
01:05:27.498 --> 01:05:32.490
So, I was sitting in my apartment one night,

1506
01:05:32.490 --> 01:05:37.490
and I get like, multiple calls in on my phone

1507
01:05:38.080 --> 01:05:39.090
one after the next I'm like

1508
01:05:39.090 --> 01:05:40.080
who is this unknown number

1509
01:05:40.080 --> 01:05:42.210
I don't understand what's going on.

1510
01:05:42.210 --> 01:05:44.490
And then I got a bunch of text messages

1511
01:05:44.490 --> 01:05:47.160
and voicemails that were left

1512
01:05:47.160 --> 01:05:50.780
and I had just retired from something

1513
01:05:50.780 --> 01:05:53.910
so literally a month retired, fresh.

1514
01:05:53.910 --> 01:05:55.940
I had been training, for context

1515
01:05:55.940 --> 01:05:59.363
at Fullerton Aquatic Club with Sean Hutchison.

1516
01:06:00.202 --> 01:06:03.030
And--
And what was the intention

1517
01:06:03.030 --> 01:06:04.270
of the training at that time?

1518
01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:07.060
'Cause this is to that is two years after the Olympics.

1519
01:06:07.060 --> 01:06:08.380
Were you thinking you're,

1520
01:06:08.380 --> 01:06:10.170
was it because you just couldn't let go of it

1521
01:06:10.170 --> 01:06:11.480
and didn't know what else to do?

1522
01:06:11.480 --> 01:06:16.480
Or did you have designs on some some lofty goals?

1523
01:06:19.820 --> 01:06:21.330
I was just running,

1524
01:06:21.330 --> 01:06:24.531
I was chasing different things

1525
01:06:24.531 --> 01:06:28.040
to check off the list that I could just do.

1526
01:06:28.040 --> 01:06:29.900
Like I needed something to feel worthy.

1527
01:06:29.900 --> 01:06:32.720
I needed something to show.

1528
01:06:32.720 --> 01:06:34.380
So I just kept doing things.

1529
01:06:34.380 --> 01:06:36.950
I was collecting accolades and degrees and all these things.

1530
01:06:36.950 --> 01:06:38.366
And I was like, oh, yeah,

1531
01:06:38.366 --> 01:06:40.250
I'll just do all these things, distract myself.

1532
01:06:40.250 --> 01:06:42.750
You're living on a Olympic in La Cienega.

1533
01:06:42.750 --> 01:06:44.598
You're driving to Fullerton to swim.

1534
01:06:44.598 --> 01:06:45.460
I mean, that's a hike.

1535
01:06:45.460 --> 01:06:47.860
Yeah, I mean, I did that just until I retired.

1536
01:06:47.860 --> 01:06:49.190
And then I was like I can't do this anymore,

1537
01:06:49.190 --> 01:06:51.558
'cause you had to transfer campus for it for Fullerton.

1538
01:06:51.558 --> 01:06:52.391
I got it.

1539
01:06:52.391 --> 01:06:56.220
But, I felt like I just had to prove myself in the sport.

1540
01:06:56.220 --> 01:06:58.940
I felt like I needed to finish it off and prove myself.

1541
01:06:58.940 --> 01:07:03.940
And when I retired, I retired at Irvine Nationals,

1542
01:07:05.473 --> 01:07:10.300
I knew my last race, I knew going into it.

1543
01:07:10.300 --> 01:07:12.570
I knew the moment I did the 200 free

1544
01:07:12.570 --> 01:07:14.030
I was in lane one, I swam horrible,

1545
01:07:14.030 --> 01:07:16.090
I got out I started, went to the diving,

1546
01:07:16.090 --> 01:07:19.020
I put my goggles on, try to not let anybody see me cry.

1547
01:07:19.020 --> 01:07:20.380
And Amanda Beard, of course,

1548
01:07:20.380 --> 01:07:22.170
comes over and puts her arm on my shoulder

1549
01:07:22.170 --> 01:07:25.430
and, she was an angel for me my whole career by the way.

1550
01:07:25.430 --> 01:07:27.980
She was an angel at the training camp

1551
01:07:27.980 --> 01:07:30.320
with some coach situations, she was an angel

1552
01:07:31.440 --> 01:07:35.350
afterward, she just said like, you're gonna be okay.

